1
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:07,335
In the last lesson, we pointed dbt at the raw taxi files.

2
00:00:07,615 --> 00:00:09,778
Now we build on top of them, with a model.

3
00:00:10,058 --> 00:00:15,496
A model is one select statement, saved in one .sql file, in the models folder.

4
00:00:16,446 --> 00:00:22,109
When you run it, dbt builds a view or a table from that select, and names it after the file.

5
00:00:22,389 --> 00:00:25,220
So trips.sql becomes a view called trips.

6
00:00:26,170 --> 00:00:28,270
Here is the project from the last lesson.

7
00:00:28,550 --> 00:00:33,542
Open it, right-click the models folder, choose New File, and call it trips.sql.

8
00:00:35,974 --> 00:00:41,869
Start with a comment that says what one row is: one green taxi trip from 2025.

9
00:00:42,149 --> 00:00:42,998
Then, select.

10
00:00:43,948 --> 00:00:51,361
Pick the columns we need, and give them clear names: the vendor, the pickup and drop-off times, and the pickup and drop-off zones.

11
00:00:52,311 --> 00:00:57,521
Then the passenger count, the distance, how the rider paid, and the money: fare, tip, and total.

12
00:00:58,471 --> 00:01:00,484
Read them from the raw green trips source.

13
00:01:00,764 --> 00:01:04,901
The source function points at the parquet files we declared in the last lesson.

14
00:01:05,851 --> 00:01:12,950
Finally, keep only trips that started in 2025, and drop the rows with no distance, or no charge.

15
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:15,144
That is the whole model.

16
00:01:15,424 --> 00:01:17,477
One select, and no create statement.

17
00:01:17,757 --> 00:01:18,374
Save it.

18
00:01:19,324 --> 00:01:20,845
So what happens when you run it?

19
00:01:21,125 --> 00:01:23,309
First, dbt compiles the file.

20
00:01:23,589 --> 00:01:29,864
The Jinja in curly braces becomes plain SQL, and the source call becomes the real read parquet call.

21
00:01:30,814 --> 00:01:34,218
Then dbt wraps your select in a create view statement.

22
00:01:34,498 --> 00:01:36,226
That is the part you never write.

23
00:01:37,176 --> 00:01:42,375
And it sends the statement to DuckDB, which saves the view in the dev database file.

24
00:01:43,325 --> 00:01:44,153
Let's run it.

25
00:01:44,433 --> 00:01:47,472
Above the editor, open the Model menu, and choose Run.

26
00:01:47,752 --> 00:01:49,127
It runs just this model.

27
00:01:50,077 --> 00:01:54,229
The terminal shows one model, trips, created as a view in the main schema.

28
00:01:54,509 --> 00:01:55,861
One pass, no errors.

29
00:01:56,811 --> 00:01:59,932
To look at the rows, use the command box under the terminal.

30
00:02:00,212 --> 00:02:05,486
The model's Preview runs outside the project folder, so it can't find the parquet files yet.

31
00:02:05,766 --> 00:02:08,655
Type: dbt show, select trips, limit ten.

32
00:02:08,935 --> 00:02:10,009
Then press Enter.

33
00:02:10,959 --> 00:02:20,485
dbt runs the model's select, and prints the first ten trips: New Year's Day, 2025, with the pickup and drop-off times and zones.

34
00:02:20,765 --> 00:02:24,323
Across the whole year, the model keeps more than half a million trips.

35
00:02:25,273 --> 00:02:28,305
Want to see exactly what dbt sent to DuckDB?

36
00:02:28,585 --> 00:02:34,920
Refresh the Explorer, and open target, then run, nyc taxi, models, and trips.sql.

37
00:02:35,870 --> 00:02:43,113
There it is: create view, followed by your select. dbt builds the view under a temporary name, then swaps it in.

38
00:02:44,063 --> 00:02:48,909
And the source call became read parquet, with the file pattern from sources.yml.

39
00:02:49,859 --> 00:02:51,461
Last step: commit and push.

40
00:02:51,741 --> 00:02:52,831
Open the Git tab.

41
00:02:53,111 --> 00:02:55,698
Trips.sql is listed as a new file.

42
00:02:55,978 --> 00:02:59,521
The target and logs folders are not, because git ignores them.

43
00:03:00,471 --> 00:03:05,540
Stage it with the plus button, type a message, add the trips staging model, and click Commit.

44
00:03:09,166 --> 00:03:12,027
Click Sync Changes, and the commit goes to GitHub.

45
00:03:14,266 --> 00:03:19,740
On GitHub, the models folder now holds trips dot sql, next to sources dot yml.

46
00:03:20,690 --> 00:03:21,423
To recap.

47
00:03:21,703 --> 00:03:33,948
A model is one select in one file. dbt compiles it, wraps it in create view, and runs it in DuckDB. dbt show prints the rows, and the Git tab sends it to GitHub.

48
00:03:34,228 --> 00:03:36,919
Next, we add two more models that build on trips.
